<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Anthony Musgrave</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1828">1828</a>–<a href="/years/1888">1888</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Anthony Musgrave was a British colonial administrator born in Antigua who governed several colonies, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/newfoundland">Newfoundland</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-columbia">British Columbia</a>, Natal, South <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamaica">Jamaica</a> and Queensland. As governor of British Columbia from 1869 he guided the colony's negotiations to join <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>, which it did in 1871. He died in office as governor of Queensland.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/anthony-musgrave.jpg" alt="Anthony Musgrave" width="320" height="430" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Anthony_Musgrave.jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Musgrave was born in St. John's, Antigua, the third of 11 children of Anthony Musgrave and Mary Harris Sheriff, into a slaveholding family: his father and uncles were compensated for their slaves after abolition in the 1830s. Educated in Antigua and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-great-britain">Great Britain</a>, he became private secretary to Robert James Mackintosh, governor-in-chief of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leeward-islands">Leeward Islands</a>, in 1854, and was soon administering Nevis and then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-vincent-and-the-grenadines">Saint Vincent and the Grenadines</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Appointed governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/newfoundland">Newfoundland</a> in September 1864, he found a colony with responsible government, an active assembly and a destitute population. He worked to bring it into the negotiations for union with the other British North American colonies, but the assembly would not accept terms, and Newfoundland stayed out of the Canadian Confederation proclaimed on 1 July 1867.</p><p>With the colonial office and Prime Minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-a-macdonald">John A. Macdonald</a> he then turned to the west. After the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-seymour">Frederick Seymour</a> he took over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-columbia">British Columbia</a> in August 1869, a colony recently united with Vancouver Island, in financial disorder and divided between Victoria and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-westminster">New Westminster</a>. He calmed its contentious assembly, and in July 1871 it became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>'s sixth province.</p><p>After a brief term in Natal he went to South Australia, where he supported the assembly's borrowing for railway building, new taxes and immigration. He then governed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamaica">Jamaica</a> for six years: the government bought the Jamaica Railway Company and extended its line, and he started the Jamaica Scholarship and helped found the Institute of Jamaica, whose Musgrave Medal was named after him in 1897.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>He arrived in Queensland on 7 November 1883 and toured the north with premier Samuel Griffith. He had to respond when Sir Thomas McIlwraith claimed to annex <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-guinea">New Guinea</a> to Queensland, a move the colonial office repudiated. About to retire, he died at his desk in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brisbane">Brisbane</a> on 9 October 1888 and was buried in Toowong General Cemetery. Port Musgrave, the Musgrave Ranges and Musgrave Harbour in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/newfoundland">Newfoundland</a> are among the places named after him.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Anthony Musgrave” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Governor <b>Anthony Musgrave</b>, appointed in 1869, steered the colony towards Confederation.</blockquote><a href="/state/colony-of-british-columbia-1866-1871">Colony of British Columbia (1866-1871)</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brisbane">Brisbane</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/australia">Australia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=-27.47,153.03&amp;year=1867" aria-label="Brisbane on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of Anthony Musgrave"><image href="/maps/globes/g150s30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5230 4787h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5230 4787h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=-27.47,153.03&amp;year=1867">What was here in 1867? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
